Dear 3DPrint.com Editors,

Solidon3D 0.3.3 targets a common desktop-printing problem that sits between
mesh repair and full CAD: the downloaded model is valid, but a functional
detail is in the wrong place.

An STL file stores triangles, not features. Solidon recognises holes, pegs,
countersinks and domes in imported geometry, and now lets a user act
on them the way CAD would: move, rotate, resize, duplicate, remove. Resizing has
been possible for a while; moving is the part that was missing. The
original axis is preserved, the edit is reversible, and the feature keeps its
identity, so a fit or a note that referred to it still refers to it
afterwards. That last point is the difference from the usual workaround of
filling a hole and drilling a new one.

Around that, 0.3.3 adds a feature panel with the measured values as editable
fields and a live preview, an option to apply one edit to every feature of the
same kind at once, and a plain-language statement of a hole's standard size
("measures 5.19 mm, the clearance hole for M5"). Recognition is limited to what
a printer can produce, which removed 296 of 1130 tree entries on a sample
downloaded part.

The application runs locally on Windows, macOS and Linux, with no account,
telemetry or cloud project store. An optional language model may call the
fixed operation API, but all geometry is calculated by deterministic code.

Overview: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Detailed changes: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

For editorial evaluation I can provide the original STL, measured
before-and-after files, the released 0.3.3 build and high-resolution images.

